                        <forename>George</forename>
                        <surname>Rogers</surname>
                        <addName/>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1000-01-01">
                        <placeName/>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1000-12-31"/>
                    <occupation>OCCUPATION</occupation>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>See Ed Pope: Rogers 10.2.1795 at King's and 22.3.1795, 3.9.1795,
                            25.12.1795, 2.2.1796, 25.3.1796, 8.4.1796, 16.5.1796, 23.3.1797,
                            2.5.1797, 28.2.1798, 8.10.1798, 15.10.1798, 27.10.1798, 7.11.1798,
                            27.5.1799, 26.10.1799, 17.11.1799, 5.1.1800, 16.3.1800 all at King's. 
                            </p>
                        <p>G.Rogers in 1796 list. George Rogers, of Audley St 1777 agent for HM ship
                            Porcupine (Sun Fire), voted 1784 Hood and Wray, 1788 Hood. 25.9.1794
                            comptroller of the Treasurer's account (Nat Arch
                            ADM359/14/194/84).19.10.1800 he wrote letter to Home Office recommending
                            John King as a magistrate (Nat Arch H) 42/52/50). Derby Mercury
                            18.6.1801 Commissioner for Navy, Somerset House, his name used by John
                            King's Portland Bank. Licence as Navy Commissioner revoked 1802. Fellow
                            of Royal Society on their lists till 1815. Currently coded on GD website
                            to Samuel Rogers, who I suspect never dined at John King's. See Scourge
                            Dec 1815 p411 which said George Rogers ended up in Fleet debtor's
                            prison. Its records (Nat Arch PRIS 1/18) show George Rogers committed
                            12.11.1801, discharged by the Insolvent Debtors Act 28.10.1811</p>
                    </note>
